CHAPTER VI — COMBAT SYSTEMS
Combat in Obsidian Protocol is a hybrid of deterministic simulation and autonomous decision‑making.
Squads evaluate risk, terrain, visibility, suppression, and intent to choose optimal actions.
This chapter defines the underlying mechanics that govern all engagements across air, ground, sea, command, and experimental units.

6.1 Initiative
Initiative determines who acts first in any engagement or combat cycle.

Initiative Sources
Unit Speed — faster units act earlier

Command Radius — units inside radius gain initiative bonuses

Elevation Advantage — high ground improves reaction time

Suppression — reduces initiative

Weather — storms and fog reduce initiative

Specialties — recon units often have initiative bonuses

Initiative Tiers
Tier I — Immediate Response (air superiority fighters, recon drones)

Tier II — Fast Response (light vehicles, scouts)

Tier III — Standard Response (infantry, medium armor)

Tier IV — Heavy Response (heavy armor, naval guns)

Tier V — Delayed Response (experimental superweapons)

Initiative is recalculated continuously during combat.

6.2 Range Bands
Range is divided into discrete bands to simplify targeting and engagement logic.

Range Bands
Melee — CQB, breach combat

Short — SMGs, shotguns, pistols

Medium — rifles, light cannons

Long — sniper rifles, tank guns

Extreme — artillery, missiles, naval guns

Range Band Effects
Accuracy modifiers

Damage falloff

Sensor effectiveness

Visibility cone interaction

Suppression potential

Range bands are essential for autonomous target selection.

6.3 Accuracy & Evasion
Accuracy determines the chance to hit; evasion determines the chance to avoid.

Accuracy Influencers
Range band

Elevation

Weather

Suppression

Movement

Weapon type

Unit specialty

Sensor lock

Evasion Influencers
Speed

Maneuverability

Cover

Smoke

Stealth

Electronic warfare

Air units rely heavily on evasion; ground units rely more on cover.

6.4 Damage Types
Damage types interact with armor, shields, and specialty defenses.

Damage Categories
Kinetic — bullets, shells

Explosive — grenades, artillery

Energy — lasers, plasma

Thermal — heat weapons, flamers

EMP — disables electronics

Shock — concussive force

Piercing — armor‑penetrating rounds

Damage Interactions
Armor reduces kinetic and explosive

Shields reduce energy and thermal

EMP bypasses armor entirely

Piercing bypasses armor partially

Shock increases suppression

Experimental units may have unique interactions.

6.5 Armor & Penetration
Armor reduces incoming damage; penetration bypasses armor.

Armor Types
Light Armor — infantry, drones

Medium Armor — vehicles, turrets

Heavy Armor — tanks, naval hulls

Superheavy Armor — experimental units

Penetration Rules
High‑caliber rounds penetrate light armor

Anti‑tank weapons penetrate medium/heavy armor

Experimental weapons may ignore armor entirely

Armor is directional — flanking reduces armor effectiveness.

6.6 Shields
Shields are energy barriers used by advanced and experimental units.

Shield Properties
Capacity — total energy

Recharge Rate — energy restored per cycle

Delay — time before recharge begins

Damage Type Interaction — energy weapons drain shields faster

Shield Break
When shields break:

Unit becomes vulnerable

Accuracy decreases

Retreat likelihood increases

AI may reposition or request support

